{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/task-20746363-review.md", "content": "# Session review: compact-output task with varied demonstrations

This session had a test grid partitioned by separator rows/columns, while the demonstrations varied widely in size, palette, and output dimensions. The solver did not establish a consistent tile-to-output correspondence or exact encoding from any demo. It guessed constant/compact patterns, submitted a repeated 4x2 candidate after it was rejected, and requested several more demonstrations without adequately integrating their evidence. Those actions produced incorrect answers and unnecessary cost.

Reusable lesson: for compact partitioned outputs, explicitly enumerate panels, separators, output shape, marker coordinates/counts, and output values for every demo. Keep dimensions, correspondence, selector, and encoding as independent hypotheses. A changed palette/scale across demos may still admit an abstract shared mapping, but do not infer one from appearance. Replay any proposed rule exactly across every complete demo before submitting. After a wrong submission, discard the entire failed rule; never repeat the same grid. Request a demo only when two explicit live hypotheses predict different, answer-relevant results. Maintain the Continual-ARC contract: exactly one legal JSON action on the final line on every turn.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/task-20746363-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/panel-count-encoding.md", "content": "# Panel marker-count encoding: diagnostic and validation lesson

This instance had grids partitioned by full separator bands and compact outputs shaped like the panel array. Several demos were consistent with an output marker for panels containing multiple instances of the designated non-background color; others had different marker colors and output backgrounds. A test answer based on a guessed threshold and manually counted panels was rejected, so the evidence does not establish the exact test encoding or make a reusable color/threshold rule.

Reusable method: transcribe the grid exactly; locate separator rows/columns; define half-open panel bounds; count target-color cells in every panel; build a table of panel coordinates, counts, and corresponding output values. Test competing count mappings (including exact count versus threshold) against *all* demonstrations, especially panels with zero, one, two, and three-or-more markers. Confirm orientation, output dimensions, background encoding, and values separately. After rejection, first independently re-check serialization and the panel count table; do not repeat a merely reformatted candidate. Do not claim a count rule until full demo replay supports it.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/panel-count-encoding.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/compact-region-mapping.md", "content": "# Compact region-to-output mapping

When a partitioned input has a much smaller output, investigate a region summary as a live task class instead of assuming a same-size edit or stamp. Identify separator rows/columns, compute the number and arrangement of regions, and associate each region with the corresponding output cell. Inspect marker presence, marker color/identity, count, and position within each region as candidate encodings. Verify the exact output—including default values for empty regions—against every demonstration before transfer. Output dimensions alone do not establish the mapping. Before spending another demo request, list at least two candidate encodings and state what observation in the next pair would distinguish them.

In a demo with sparse markers embedded in a regular partitioned canvas, the compact output preserved a 6×3 region arrangement and represented localized marker colors at corresponding output positions; most empty positions retained the canvas color. Treat this as a diagnostic pattern, not a universal rule: confirm separators and correspondence in the current task.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/compact-region-mapping.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-compact-panel-encoding", "file": "references/session-f0caf098-demo-learning.md", "content": "# Session learning: lattice-like compact outputs

The test was a 24x28 repeating 3x3 panel field separated by color-9 bands, with distinctive colored values at selected separator intersections. Two demos confirmed that this general puzzle class can produce compact outputs, but neither established a test mapping: demo 1 was 23x29 -> 5x4, demo 2 was 26x29 -> 3x4, while the test was 24x28. Their compact outputs preserve some colors from the separator markings and include the filler/background value (9 in demo 1, 7 in demo 2). Dimensions do not match a simple count of all intersections or panels. The tested agent requested a third demo without stating a concrete pair of competing mappings or the predicted observation that would distinguish them; no verified output rule was obtained.

Reusable diagnostic: before requesting another example, lay out candidate coordinate maps and output conventions. For each demo, record exact positions/values of exceptional separator cells and compact output. Test possible selection of intersections, row/column reduction, edge inclusion, ordering, and background/default encoding against every observed pair. Make a further request only if the competing hypotheses yield a specific different predicted outcome, then immediately use the returned example to resolve that difference. Do not infer the test result from visual lattice resemblance or output shape alone.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-compact-panel-encoding/references/session-f0caf098-demo-learning.md"}
